    "changelog": "<h4>Changed</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>**The pre-search category selector no longer carries the whole catalogue tree.** Every search form printed the full hierarchical list of categories, in every form, on every page of the site — and a header search bar plus one in the page body means the same list twice. Measured on a shop of 443 product categories: 438 `<option>` and 43 920 bytes of markup per selector, 87 840 bytes on the home page — two thirds of the page's HTML — 876 DOM nodes the browser builds before anything is searched, and 48 ms of PHP spent rendering a list per view. The form now ships with \"All categories\" and, when the address carries one, the current choice; the tree itself comes from the new `mvweb-ss/v1/categories` route, once per visitor, cached on both sides — the same list is 30 472 bytes of JSON, 5 069 compressed. It is filled while the page is idle rather than on click, because a native `<select>` freezes its list the moment the picker opens and a fetch started by the tap would leave the first one empty. Without JavaScript the form still submits and still honours a category already in the address. The selector is off by default and unchanged for anyone who leaves it off.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Fixed</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>**A part number typed on its own now finds its part.** A short code — `bn5p`, `bp4e`, `lj6` — returned a hundred unrelated rows without the product it names anywhere among them. The search did find it, then threw the answer away: a one- or two-row result counts as \"almost nothing\", which starts the wrong-keyboard-layout rescue, and the rescue keeps whatever result is *larger*. Retyped in the other layout the code becomes a nonsense word that falls apart into a stray letter and a bare digit, and that digit matches half a parts catalogue — a hundred rows beat two, so two correct products lost to a hundred wrong ones. A model the catalogue does not carry is now a requirement for itself rather than for any scrap of itself, so the nonsense variant matches nothing and is never adopted. Measured on a 14 700-product catalogue over every such code in it: 62 codes returned 6103 rows with 8 relevant ones and 56 codes showing nothing they named; the same 62 now return 73 rows, all 73 relevant, none broken. The suggestions dropdown is covered too — there the word under the cursor stays optional, as a half-typed code should, but it is no longer allowed to stand in for itself in pieces. And a query that names a model or a part number and finds something is no longer handed to the rescues at all: rewriting it — into the other keyboard layout, or word by word for typos — returns more rows almost by definition, so six neighbouring codes used to outnumber the one exact answer and bury it under a \"showing results for BN52\" banner. Measured in dropdown mode over the same 62 codes: the code asked for is now the first row for all 62, and none of them has its query rewritten. Typo correction is untouched for queries made of ordinary words.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Changed</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>**A model modifier is treated as part of the model's name.** \"M3\" and \"M3 Pro\", \"13\" and \"13 mini\" are different phones whose parts do not interchange, but only the number was protected: the modifier carries no digit, so when the search loosened its demands it dropped exactly the word that decides which phone was meant, and a query for an M3 Pro screen answered with M3 screens. Modifiers following a model number (`pro`, `max`, `plus`, `ultra`, `lite`, `mini`, `neo`, `air`, `fe`, `se`; anything with a digit in it, like `5g`, was already covered) are now demanded alongside it, and a store with its own vocabulary can adjust the list through the `mvweb_ss_model_qualifiers` filter. Measured over 40 model-and-modifier pairs from a 14 700-product catalogue: 344 rows of which 5 lack the modifier, and no query left without an answer. Asking without the modifier is unchanged — it still shows every variant, which is what a shopper who did not specify one is asking for.</li>\n<li>**A modifier the catalogue never carried returns nothing rather than the neighbouring model.** The last resort of a search that found nothing is to drop the model and answer with whatever matched — sensible for a model nobody stocks, wrong for a modifier: a shopper asking for \"M3 Pro\" and shown an M3 cannot tell from the listing that the part fits a different phone. That last resort now keeps the modifier, so the query honestly comes up empty and the dropdown offers best-sellers instead.</li>\n</ul>"
  }
